// FiqhHub — Nikah (Marriage) Fatwas
window.FATWAS_NIKAH = [
{id:'n001',cat:'nikah',sub:'Contract',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for a valid Islamic marriage contract?',a:'A valid nikah (marriage contract) requires: (1) Offer (ijab) — stated by the bride or her representative: "Zawwajtuka nafsi" (I marry myself to you). (2) Acceptance (qabul) — stated by the groom: "Qabiltu" (I accept). (3) Both parties must specify the mahr (dowry). (4) The formula should ideally be in Arabic, though some scholars allow other languages if Arabic is not understood. (5) Both parties must be sane and of legal age. (6) The marriage must be free of impediments (mahram relationships, iddah period, etc.). (7) Consent of both parties is essential. Witnesses are recommended but not a condition for validity in Shia fiqh.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin, Vol 2, Nikah',tags:['nikah','contract','conditions','marriage','validity']},
{id:'n002',cat:'nikah',sub:'Consent',marja:'sistani',q:'Is the father\'s permission required for a virgin girl\'s marriage?',a:'According to the obligatory precaution, the permission of the father (or paternal grandfather) is required for the marriage of a virgin girl, even if she is an adult. However, if the father unreasonably withholds permission — for example, refusing all suitable proposals without valid shariah reason — his permission is no longer required. If the father is absent, uncontactable, or has abandoned the family, his permission is likewise not required. For a previously married woman (non-virgin), the father\'s permission is not needed. This ruling aims to protect the girl\'s interests while ensuring parental involvement.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Ruling 2377',tags:['nikah','consent','father','virgin','permission']},
{id:'n003',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'khamenei',q:'What can be specified as mahr (dowry)?',a:'The mahr can be anything of value that the couple agrees upon: money, gold, property, goods, or even a service (like teaching Quran). There is no minimum or maximum, though moderation is recommended following the Prophetic tradition — the best marriage is one with the lightest mahr. The mahr is the wife\'s exclusive right. It can be paid immediately (mu\'ajjal) or deferred (mu\'ajjal). If no mahr is specified in the contract, the marriage is still valid, and the wife is entitled to mahr al-mithl (the customary mahr for a woman of her status). The husband is obligated to pay the mahr on demand.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahr','dowry','amount','rights','wife']},
{id:'n004',cat:'nikah',sub:'Temporary Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'What is mut\'ah (temporary marriage) and what are its conditions?',a:'Mut\'ah (temporary marriage) is a legitimate marriage in Shia Islam with a specified duration. Its conditions include: (1) A specific time period must be set (hours, days, months, years). (2) A mahr must be specified. (3) The formula is: she says "Zawwajtuka nafsi fil-muddatil-ma\'lumah alal-mahril-ma\'lum" and he says "Qabiltu." (4) There must be no impediments (mahram, iddah, etc.). (5) A Muslim woman cannot contract mut\'ah with a non-Muslim man. (6) As an obligatory precaution, a Muslim man should not contract mut\'ah with a non-Kitabi non-Muslim woman. After the period expires, the marriage ends automatically. The woman must observe iddah of two menstrual cycles (or 45 days).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin, Vol 2',tags:['mutah','temporary','marriage','conditions','time']},
{id:'n005',cat:'nikah',sub:'Prohibited Marriages',marja:'makarem',q:'Who is permanently prohibited (mahram) for marriage?',a:'Permanently prohibited marriage partners include: (1) Blood relations: mother, daughter, sister, paternal aunt, maternal aunt, brother\'s daughter, sister\'s daughter (and their male equivalents). (2) Relations through marriage: mother-in-law (permanently after marriage), daughter-in-law (son\'s wife), stepdaughter (if marriage to her mother was consummated), stepmother (father\'s wife). (3) Relations through breastfeeding: a woman who breastfed you and her relations become mahram under specific conditions (the child must have been breastfed 15 consecutive times or for a full day and night from one woman, within the first two years of birth). These prohibitions are permanent and can never be married.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Nikah prohibitions',tags:['mahram','prohibited','marriage','blood','breastfeeding']},
{id:'n006',cat:'nikah',sub:'Interfaith Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'Can a Muslim man marry a Christian or Jewish woman?',a:'A Muslim man may marry a Christian or Jewish (Ahlul Kitab) woman in a permanent marriage, though it is makruh (discouraged). For temporary marriage (mut\'ah) with an Ahlul Kitab woman, it is permissible according to many scholars. However, a Muslim woman is absolutely prohibited from marrying any non-Muslim man, whether Ahlul Kitab or otherwise — this prohibition is by consensus (ijma\'). If a non-Muslim husband converts to Islam but his wife is Ahlul Kitab, the marriage continues. If neither spouse is Muslim and the wife later converts but the husband does not, specific waiting period rules apply to determine if the marriage continues.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin, Vol 2',tags:['interfaith','marriage','christian','jewish','ahlul kitab']},
{id:'n007',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the wife\'s financial rights in marriage?',a:'The wife has several financial rights: (1) Mahr — the agreed dowry, payable on demand. (2) Nafaqah — the husband is obligated to provide maintenance including housing appropriate to her status, food, clothing, medical expenses, and household items. This obligation exists regardless of the wife\'s own wealth. (3) She retains full ownership of her own property, earnings, and inheritance — the husband has no right over her personal wealth. (4) She may work and keep her earnings. (5) If the husband fails to provide nafaqah, she can seek it through the Islamic court and may be permitted to take from his wealth what is customary. These rights apply in permanent marriage; temporary marriage has different nafaqah rules.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['wife','rights','financial','nafaqah','mahr']},
{id:'n008',cat:'nikah',sub:'Contract',marja:'shirazi',q:'Can conditions be added to the marriage contract?',a:'Yes, both parties can stipulate conditions in the marriage contract, provided the conditions do not contradict the essence of marriage or Islamic law. Valid conditions include: (1) The wife stipulating the right to divorce under certain circumstances. (2) The wife stipulating the right to continue education or work. (3) Specifying the place of residence. (4) Stipulating that the husband will not marry a second wife. (5) Financial arrangements. If a valid condition is violated, the other party has the right to annul the marriage. Conditions that are against shariah (e.g., no inheritance rights) are void but do not invalidate the marriage itself.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Marriage conditions',tags:['nikah','conditions','stipulations','contract','rights']},
{id:'n009',cat:'nikah',sub:'Second Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for marrying a second wife?',a:'Islam permits a man to have up to four permanent wives simultaneously, but with strict conditions: (1) He must be able to treat all wives with justice and equality in terms of financial provision, time, and treatment. (2) He must be financially capable of providing for multiple households. (3) If he stipulated in the first marriage contract not to marry again, he is bound by that condition. (4) While the first wife\'s permission is not technically a shariah requirement, treating her justly includes considering her feelings and wellbeing. Marrying a second wife while unable to maintain justice is sinful. The Quranic verse "if you fear you cannot be just, then only one" (4:3) emphasizes the difficulty of this justice.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['polygamy','second wife','conditions','justice']},
{id:'n010',cat:'nikah',sub:'Contract',marja:'hakim',q:'Is it valid to conduct a marriage contract online or by phone?',a:'A marriage contract conducted by phone, video call, or online platforms can be valid if the essential elements are present: both parties clearly hear each other, the offer and acceptance are expressed in proper form, and the mahr is specified. The key is that both parties (or their representatives) clearly communicate and understand the terms. Written agreements alone (text messages, emails) are not sufficient — the verbal formula of ijab and qabul must be pronounced. Having a scholar or knowledgeable person facilitate the contract and witnesses present (even via video) is strongly recommended to avoid disputes.',source:'Misbah al-Minhaj',tags:['nikah','online','phone','distance','validity']},
{id:'n011',cat:'nikah',sub:'Temporary Marriage',marja:'makarem',q:'Does the woman need her father\'s permission for mut\'ah?',a:'The same ruling that applies to permanent marriage applies to temporary marriage regarding the father\'s permission. For a virgin girl (who has never been married before), the permission of her father or paternal grandfather is required as an obligatory precaution, even for mut\'ah. If the father\'s permission cannot be obtained (he is absent, refusing without reason, or deceased), the same exemptions apply. For a previously married woman, whether divorced or widowed, the father\'s permission is not required for mut\'ah. Some scholars are more lenient regarding mut\'ah specifically, but the cautious position requires permission for virgins.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mutah','permission','father','virgin','temporary']},
{id:'n012',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'wahid',q:'What are the husband\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The husband\'s primary rights include: (1) Tamkin — the wife should be available for marital intimacy when the husband reasonably desires, unless she has a legitimate excuse (illness, menstruation, obligatory fasting, etc.). (2) The wife should not leave the marital home without the husband\'s permission for non-essential outings, though this does not apply to emergency situations, obligatory acts (like Hajj), visiting parents within reason, or fulfilling her own rights. (3) Not admitting anyone into the home whom the husband objects to. These rights are reciprocal — the husband also has obligations including kind treatment, financial provision, and respecting the wife\'s dignity and independence.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['husband','rights','tamkin','obligations']},
{id:'n013',cat:'nikah',sub:'Engagement',marja:'najafi',q:'What is the ruling on engagement before marriage?',a:'Engagement (khutbah) is a promise to marry and is separate from the actual marriage contract. During engagement: (1) The couple are not yet married — rules of non-mahram still apply. (2) Looking at the prospective spouse\'s face and hands is permitted for the purpose of deciding on marriage. (3) Being alone together (khalwah) is not permissible. (4) The engagement can be broken by either party without sin, though it is discourteous to break it without valid reason. (5) Gifts given during engagement are generally considered gifts and need not be returned if the engagement is broken, unless there was an explicit condition. (6) It is recommended to pray istikhara and consult family.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['engagement','khutbah','looking','halal','rules']},
{id:'n014',cat:'nikah',sub:'Contract',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the iddah period after permanent marriage ends?',a:'The iddah (waiting period) varies: (1) Divorce: 3 menstrual cycles (or 3 months for those who don\'t menstruate). (2) Death of husband: 4 months and 10 days (even if the marriage was not consummated). (3) If pregnant: the iddah extends until delivery, in both divorce and death cases. (4) Temporary marriage (mut\'ah) ending: 2 menstrual cycles (or 45 days). (5) If mut\'ah was not consummated: no iddah. (6) Menopausal woman after divorce: 3 months. During iddah of death, the wife must avoid adornment and certain activities. During iddah of revocable divorce, the husband can return without a new contract.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin, Iddah',tags:['iddah','waiting period','divorce','death','duration']},
{id:'n015',cat:'nikah',sub:'Breastfeeding',marja:'makarem',q:'What conditions make breastfeeding create mahram relations?',a:'Breastfeeding (rada\'ah) establishes mahram relationships under these conditions: (1) The child must be breastfed at least 15 consecutive feedings without interruption by another food source, or for a full 24-hour period exclusively. (2) The milk must be from one woman who became pregnant from one man. (3) The breastfeeding must occur within the first two years of the child\'s life. (4) The child must drink directly from the breast (not expressed/bottled milk according to most scholars). (5) Each feeding must be full (the baby feeds until satisfied and detaches). When these conditions are met, the nursed child becomes mahram to the breastfeeding woman, her husband, and their relatives.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail, Rada\'ah',tags:['breastfeeding','mahram','conditions','radaah']},
{id:'nk016',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'sistani',q:'What is mahr (dowry) and what are its rules?',a:'Mahr is the wife\'s absolute right — a gift from the husband specified in the marriage contract. Rules: (1) It can be anything of value — money, property, gold, even teaching Quran. (2) It becomes the wife\'s exclusive property — the husband has no right to it. (3) It can be paid immediately (mu\'ajjal) or deferred (mu\'ajjal). (4) The wife can demand immediate payment at any time. (5) She can withhold marital relations until the mahr is paid (if it was meant to be immediate). (6) If no mahr is specified, the marriage is valid and she receives mahr al-mithl (comparable mahr).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mahr','dowry','marriage','wife','right','payment']},
{id:'nk017',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is mahr (dowry) and what are its rules?',a:'Mahr is the wife\'s financial right from the marriage contract. The Sunnah encourages reasonable mahr — the Prophet (s) said: "The best women of my ummah are those with the lightest mahr." Excessive mahr can create financial burden and social pressure. The mahr can be in any permissible form: gold coins, money, property, or even non-material things like memorizing Quran. It is fully the wife\'s property and she can dispose of it as she wishes.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahr','dowry','marriage','wife','right','payment']},
{id:'nk018',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'makarem',q:'What is mahr (dowry) and what are its rules?',a:'Mahr is both a legal right and a symbol of the husband\'s commitment. Modern considerations: (1) Extremely high mahr that the husband cannot realistically pay creates problems — the Sunnah encourages moderation. (2) The wife can forgive part or all of the mahr voluntarily — but no pressure should be applied. (3) In case of divorce, the wife keeps the full mahr if the marriage was consummated, and half if divorced before consummation. (4) Currency fluctuations may affect the value of mahr set in specific amounts — some scholars advise tying it to gold.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['mahr','dowry','marriage','wife','right','payment']},
{id:'nk019',cat:'nikah',sub:'Conditions',marja:'sistani',q:'Can a woman set conditions in the marriage contract?',a:'Yes, a woman can stipulate conditions in the marriage contract (aqd), and if the husband agrees, these become binding. Valid conditions include: (1) Right to education/employment, (2) Right to choose residence location, (3) Right to travel, (4) Financial conditions, (5) Delegation of divorce right (tawkil), (6) Any condition that doesn\'t contradict the nature of marriage. If the husband violates an agreed condition, the wife may have grounds for annulment depending on the condition. Conditions should be clearly stated and documented.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['conditions','marriage','contract','wife','rights','stipulation']},
{id:'nk020',cat:'nikah',sub:'Conditions',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can a woman set conditions in the marriage contract?',a:'Marriage conditions stipulated in the contract are legally binding. In the Islamic Republic, standard marriage contracts include several pre-printed conditions that can be checked, including: the wife\'s right to divorce if the husband takes a second wife without her consent, right to half the husband\'s wealth in case of divorce, and right to education and employment. Additional conditions can be added. These contractual rights protect women within the Islamic legal framework.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['conditions','marriage','contract','wife','rights','stipulation']},
{id:'nk021',cat:'nikah',sub:'Conditions',marja:'makarem',q:'Can a woman set conditions in the marriage contract?',a:'Setting conditions in the marriage contract is the woman\'s right and is encouraged for preventing future disputes. The conditions must be: (1) Agreed upon by both parties, (2) Documented in the contract, (3) Not contradicting Islamic law, (4) Not contradicting the fundamental rights and obligations of marriage. Example valid conditions: right to continue education, right to work in a specific field, residence in a particular city, financial transparency between spouses. Example invalid condition: no marital relations at all (contradicts marriage essence).',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['conditions','marriage','contract','wife','rights','stipulation']},
{id:'nk022',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mut\'a',marja:'sistani',q:'What is mut\'a (temporary marriage) and what are its conditions?',a:'Mut\'a (temporary marriage) is permissible in Shia Islam based on Quran (4:24) and hadith. Conditions: (1) Specific formula must be recited (sighah), (2) A specific time period must be agreed upon, (3) A specific mahr must be set, (4) The woman must not be currently married, (5) She must not be in iddah, (6) A Muslim woman can do mut\'a with a Muslim man; a Muslim man can do mut\'a with a Muslim or Ahl al-Kitab woman, (7) A virgin girl needs her father\'s permission on obligatory precaution. The temporary wife has right to mahr but not nafaqah (maintenance) unless stipulated. Children from mut\'a are legitimate with full inheritance rights.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['muta','temporary','marriage','conditions','permissible','time']},
{id:'nk023',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mut\'a',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is mut\'a (temporary marriage) and what are its conditions?',a:'Mut\'a is halal and has legal framework. It was practiced during the Prophet\'s time and only Umar banned it — Shia scholars consider that ban invalid as it contradicts the Quran. Conditions include mutual consent, specified duration, specified mahr, and adherence to all Islamic ethics. It is not a casual arrangement — it carries responsibilities. The woman observes iddah after the period ends (2 menstrual cycles or 45 days). Children have full legal rights. Both parties should approach it with seriousness and moral responsibility.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['muta','temporary','marriage','conditions','permissible','time']},
{id:'nk024',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mut\'a',marja:'makarem',q:'What is mut\'a (temporary marriage) and what are its conditions?',a:'Mut\'a is permitted but should be understood properly: it is a legal framework with specific rules, not a loophole for casual relationships. Practical guidance: (1) The formula must be recited (the woman says "zawwajtuka nafsi..." with the period and mahr specified), (2) Both parties must be eligible, (3) The iddah must be observed, (4) Children are 100% legitimate, (5) It provides a halal option in situations where permanent marriage is not feasible. Cultural stigma around mut\'a does not change its legal permissibility.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['muta','temporary','marriage','conditions','permissible','time']},
{id:'nk025',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the husband\'s obligations toward his wife?',a:'The husband is obligated to provide: (1) Nafaqah — financial maintenance including housing appropriate to her social status, food, clothing, medical care, and all reasonable expenses. (2) Fair treatment (mu\'asharat bil ma\'ruf) — kindness, respect, and good companionship as commanded in Quran (4:19). (3) Sexual relations — he must not neglect her needs for more than 4 months. (4) Not harming her physically, verbally, or emotionally. (5) Permitting her to visit her family within reasonable limits. The wife does not need to contribute financially even if she is wealthy.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['husband','obligations','nafaqah','maintenance','rights','wife']},
{id:'nk026',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the husband\'s obligations toward his wife?',a:'A husband must provide comprehensive maintenance regardless of the wife\'s own wealth. This includes: suitable housing (she is entitled to a separate home from his family if she demands it), food, clothing, and medical care at a standard appropriate to her status. He must treat her with kindness, consult her on family matters, not restrict her unreasonably, and create a loving home environment. The Prophet (s) said: "The best of you is the one who is best to his family."',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['husband','obligations','nafaqah','maintenance','rights','wife']},
{id:'nk027',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the husband\'s obligations toward his wife?',a:'The husband\'s duties are extensive: financial support (housing, food, clothing, medical, personal expenses), emotional support (companionship, affection, attention), spiritual partnership (encouraging each other in faith), and physical needs. He may not force her to do housework — if she does so, it is voluntary and praiseworthy. He must not abuse her in any form. Imam Sadiq (as) said: "I am amazed at a man who beats his wife — he is more deserving of being beaten himself."',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['husband','obligations','nafaqah','maintenance','rights','wife']},
{id:'nk028',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the wife\'s obligations toward her husband?',a:'The wife\'s primary obligation is tamkin — making herself available for marital relations when the husband reasonably desires, unless she has a valid excuse (illness, haydh, obligatory act of worship). She should not leave the home without his permission for non-essential outings (scholars differ on the scope of this). She should not admit anyone into the home whom he disapproves of. Importantly: she is NOT obligated to cook, clean, or do housework — if she does, it is voluntary. She retains full control of her own finances and property.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['wife','obligations','tamkin','rights','marriage','husband']},
{id:'nk029',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the wife\'s obligations toward her husband?',a:'The wife\'s obligations center on: (1) Not denying marital relations without valid excuse, (2) Not leaving the home for extended periods without consultation, (3) Maintaining the sanctity and privacy of the marriage. These obligations are balanced by her extensive rights. She maintains her own financial independence — her earnings, inheritance, and mahr are entirely hers. She does not need to cook, clean, or serve the household — though mutual cooperation makes a better home.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['wife','obligations','tamkin','rights','marriage','husband']},
{id:'nk030',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the wife\'s obligations toward her husband?',a:'In practice, a successful marriage is built on mutual respect, not a list of demands. The technical obligations exist as a framework, but the Prophetic model shows: the Prophet (s) helped with housework, mended his own clothes, and treated his wives as partners. Both spouses should focus on fulfilling the other\'s rights rather than demanding their own. The strongest marriages in Islamic tradition are those built on love (mawaddah) and mercy (rahmah) as the Quran describes.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['wife','obligations','tamkin','rights','marriage','husband']},
{id:'nk031',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mixed Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'Can a Shia Muslim marry a Sunni Muslim?',a:'Marriage between Shia and Sunni Muslims is permissible — both are Muslim. However, there may be practical challenges regarding religious education of children, observation of different fiqhi rulings, and family harmony. It is recommended to discuss these matters before marriage. There is no fiqhi prohibition on Shia-Sunni marriage. The key concerns are practical: whose marja will the family follow? How will children be raised? These should be resolved through mutual agreement.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mixed','sunni','shia','marriage','interfaith','permissible']},
{id:'nk032',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mixed Marriage',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can a Shia Muslim marry a Sunni Muslim?',a:'Shia-Sunni marriage is permissible and can even be beneficial for Muslim unity. There is no religious prohibition. The important factor is compatibility and mutual respect for each other\'s practices. Families should discuss upfront: prayer style, children\'s education, celebration of events, etc. The bond of Islam unites all Muslims, and marriage across schools of thought strengthens the ummah when approached with maturity and respect.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mixed','sunni','shia','marriage','interfaith','permissible']},
{id:'nk033',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mixed Marriage',marja:'makarem',q:'Can a Shia Muslim marry a Sunni Muslim?',a:'There is no prohibition on Shia-Sunni marriages. The Prophet (s) said: "If someone whose religion and character please you proposes, accept." The standard is faith and character, not specific school of thought. Practical advice: discuss differences openly before marriage, agree on how to raise children, respect each other\'s practices, and focus on shared Islamic values rather than sectarian differences.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['mixed','sunni','shia','marriage','interfaith','permissible']},
{id:'nk034',cat:'nikah',sub:'Prohibited Marriages',marja:'sistani',q:'Who is one prohibited from marrying (mahram)?',a:'Permanent marriage prohibitions: (1) By blood (nasab): mother/father, daughter/son, sister/brother, paternal aunt/uncle, maternal aunt/uncle, niece/nephew, grandparents and grandchildren. (2) By marriage (musaharah): mother-in-law/father-in-law (permanently), step-daughter if marriage was consummated, daughter-in-law. (3) By breastfeeding (ridha\'ah): the same relatives as blood become mahram through substantial breastfeeding (specific conditions on quantity and timing). (4) Combining: cannot marry two sisters simultaneously, cannot marry a woman and her aunt simultaneously.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mahram','prohibited','marriage','blood','breastfeeding','relatives']},
{id:'nk035',cat:'nikah',sub:'Prohibited Marriages',marja:'khamenei',q:'Who is one prohibited from marrying (mahram)?',a:'The mahram categories create clear boundaries for family interaction. These are permanent prohibitions with no exceptions. Additionally, marrying certain combinations simultaneously is forbidden: two sisters, a woman and her maternal/paternal aunt. After divorce, one can marry the ex-spouse\'s sister once the iddah is complete. Foster/breastfeeding relationships create mahramiyyah equivalent to blood — this has significant social implications in traditional societies where wet-nursing was common.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahram','prohibited','marriage','blood','breastfeeding','relatives']},

{id:'nk036',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'sistani',q:'What is mahr and is there a limit?',a:'Mahr (dowry) is the wife\'s absolute right given by the husband — it is not optional. It can be any amount of money or valuable property that the wife agrees to. There is no set minimum or maximum in Islamic law, though the Prophet (s) discouraged excessive mahr that burdens the husband. The mahr becomes the wife\'s personal property — the husband, her father, and no one else has any claim to it. It can be paid at the time of marriage or deferred (mahr mu\'ajjal vs mu\'ajjal).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mahr','dowry','wife','marriage','payment']},
{id:'nk037',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is mahr and is there a limit?',a:'Mahr is obligatory and belongs exclusively to the wife. It can be: (1) Prompt (mu\'ajjal) — paid at marriage, (2) Deferred (mu\'ajjal) — payable on demand or at specified time, (3) A combination. The wife can demand the deferred mahr at any time and can refuse to consummate the marriage until it is paid (if it was agreed as prompt). Excessive mahr that the husband clearly cannot pay is discouraged. Common cultural practices of inflating mahr can cause problems and are not Islamic.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahr','dowry','wife','marriage','payment']},
{id:'nk038',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'makarem',q:'What is mahr and is there a limit?',a:'Mahr demonstrates respect for the wife and is her security. The best mahr is one that is reasonable and fair. The Prophet\'s (s) daughter Fatimah (sa) had a modest mahr. Excessively high mahr can: burden the husband, prevent marriages, and create conflict if divorce occurs. Both families should approach mahr discussion with wisdom and moderation. Ultimately, the amount is agreed between the couple.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['mahr','dowry','wife','marriage','payment']},
{id:'nk039',cat:'nikah',sub:'Temporary Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'What is mut\'ah (temporary marriage)?',a:'Mut\'ah is a legitimate marriage contract with a specified duration and mahr. It was permitted by the Prophet (s) and remains valid in Shia fiqh — the Shia position is that it was never abrogated. Conditions: (1) Both parties must consent, (2) A mahr must be specified, (3) A time period must be specified, (4) The woman must not be currently married, (5) Iddah must be observed after. It provides a halal framework for relationships. Children from mut\'ah have full legal rights. The woman should not be from a background where it would cause her social harm.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['mutah','temporary','marriage','permissible','quran']},
{id:'nk040',cat:'nikah',sub:'Temporary Marriage',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is mut\'ah (temporary marriage)?',a:'Mut\'ah is a Quranically sanctioned form of marriage (4:24) that the Prophet (s) permitted. It was Umar who prohibited it, not the Prophet. Conditions are strict: specific duration, agreed mahr, the woman\'s consent, and she must observe iddah afterward. It is not casual — it is a real marriage with rights and responsibilities. Children are legitimate with full inheritance rights. It serves as a halal alternative to haram relationships in various social situations.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mutah','temporary','marriage','permissible','quran']},
{id:'nk041',cat:'nikah',sub:'Temporary Marriage',marja:'makarem',q:'What is mut\'ah (temporary marriage)?',a:'Mut\'ah is a legitimate Islamic marriage that differs from permanent marriage only in having a specified duration and some simplified procedures. It has full legal standing. The stigma attached to it in some cultures is not from Islamic teachings. However, it should not be misused or treated casually. It is appropriate for situations where permanent marriage is not feasible: students, travelers, widows/divorcees. The woman\'s dignity and rights must be fully protected.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail / Istifta\'at',tags:['mutah','temporary','marriage','permissible','quran']},
{id:'nk042',cat:'nikah',sub:'Wife\'s Rights',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the wife\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The wife\'s rights include: (1) Mahr — her exclusive property, (2) Nafaqah — the husband must provide food, clothing, housing, and medical care appropriate to his means, (3) Kind treatment — "Live with them in kindness" (4:19), (4) Intimacy rights — the husband should not neglect his wife, (5) Freedom to manage her own wealth, (6) Right to work and earn with reasonable consideration of family duties, (7) Education rights, (8) Right to visit her family. Any form of physical or emotional abuse is absolutely haram.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin / Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['wife','rights','marriage','nafaqah','treatment']},
{id:'nk043',cat:'nikah',sub:'Wife\'s Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the wife\'s rights in marriage?',a:'Islam grants comprehensive rights to wives: financial maintenance without condition (even if she is wealthy), respectful treatment, emotional support, shared decision-making in family matters, freedom to maintain her personal wealth, right to education, right to work (with mutual agreement), and protection from harm. The Prophet (s) said: "The best of you is the best to his family, and I am the best to my family." Domestic violence is categorically forbidden.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['wife','rights','marriage','nafaqah','treatment']},

{id:'nk044',cat:'nikah',sub:'Husband Rights',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the husband\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The husband\'s rights include: (1) Obedience in matters related to marital life — specifically, the wife should not leave the house without his permission for non-obligatory matters (this right is debated in scope by scholars), (2) Intimacy rights within reasonable bounds, (3) Respect and kind treatment, (4) Maintaining the household (though not an absolute obligation on the wife). These rights are balanced by his extensive obligations: nafaqah, mahr, kind treatment, justice, and emotional support. Marriage is a partnership, not a hierarchy.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['husband','rights','marriage','qiwamah','obligations']},
{id:'nk045',cat:'nikah',sub:'Husband Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the husband\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The husband has certain rights related to family management and intimacy, balanced by his comprehensive obligations. The Quran describes spouses as "garments for each other" (2:187) — indicating mutual protection, comfort, and support. The husband\'s right to family management (qiwamah - 4:34) comes with the obligation to provide, protect, and decide with consultation (shura). It is not a license for control or abuse.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['husband','rights','marriage','qiwamah','obligations']},
{id:'nk046',cat:'nikah',sub:'Compatibility',marja:'sistani',q:'What factors should be considered when choosing a spouse?',a:'Islam emphasizes several factors: (1) Religious commitment (deen) — the Prophet (s) said: "When someone whose religion and character pleases you comes to you [seeking marriage], then marry [your daughter to] him." (2) Good character (akhlaq), (3) Compatibility in social standing (kafa\'ah), (4) Physical attraction and health, (5) Family background, (6) Education and intellectual compatibility. Wealth alone should not be the primary criterion. Both families should investigate the other party through trustworthy mutual connections before finalizing.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['spouse','choosing','compatibility','character','religion','marriage']},
{id:'nk047',cat:'nikah',sub:'Compatibility',marja:'makarem',q:'What factors should be considered when choosing a spouse?',a:'The Prophet (s) prioritized religious commitment and character above all. Additional considerations: emotional maturity, shared values and life goals, communication compatibility, willingness to fulfill marital duties, mental health, and family support. Islamic istikharah (seeking divine guidance) is recommended after doing due diligence. Meeting the potential spouse (within Islamic guidelines) is encouraged to assess compatibility. Rushing into marriage without proper investigation is unwise.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['spouse','choosing','compatibility','character','religion','marriage']},
{id:'nk048',cat:'nikah',sub:'Compatibility',marja:'noori',q:'What factors should be considered when choosing a spouse?',a:'Choosing a spouse is one of life\'s most important decisions. The hadith literature provides comprehensive guidance: seek the person of deen and akhlaq first. Imam Sadiq (as) advised looking at the potential spouse\'s relationship with their parents as an indicator of character. Practical compatibility (financial expectations, lifestyle, where to live, family planning) should also be discussed openly before commitment.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['spouse','choosing','compatibility','character','religion','marriage']},
,
{id:'nk049',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on mahr and its types?',a:'Mahr is the wife\'s absolute right and must be specified in the marriage contract. It can be anything of value: money, property, gold, teaching Quran, or any agreed benefit. Mahr al-musamma is the specified amount. If not specified, mahr al-mithl (equivalent to what women of similar status receive) applies. The husband must pay it on demand — delay without the wife\'s consent is sinful. It is entirely the wife\'s property.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['mahr','right','property','specified']},
{id:'nk050',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on mahr and its types?',a:'Mahr is a fundamental right of the wife established by the Quran. It can be paid immediately or deferred. The wife can refuse consummation until mahr is paid. It remains her personal property — she has no obligation to spend it on household expenses. Extremely high mahrs that the husband cannot realistically pay are discouraged as they defeat the purpose. Moderation is recommended.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahr','quran','deferred','moderation']},
{id:'nk051',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the wife\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The wife has numerous rights: (1) Mahr, (2) Financial maintenance (nafaqah) — housing, food, clothing appropriate to her status, (3) Kind treatment, (4) Fair division of nights in polygamous marriages, (5) Right to sexual relations, (6) Right to visit her family, (7) Right to her own property and earnings, (8) Right to seek divorce under certain conditions. These rights are non-negotiable and cannot be waived under pressure.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['wife','rights','nafaqah','treatment']},
{id:'nk052',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the husband\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The husband has rights including: (1) The wife should not refuse intimate relations without valid reason, (2) She should not leave the house without his permission for non-essential outings (scholars debate the scope of this), (3) She should not allow anyone he disapproves of into the home, (4) Mutual respect and kindness. These rights are balanced by his obligations of maintenance, kind treatment, and fulfilling her rights.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['husband','rights','balanced','obligations']},
{id:'nk053',cat:'nikah',sub:'Compatibility',marja:'makarem',q:'What factors should be considered when choosing a spouse?',a:'Islamic guidance emphasizes: religious commitment and character above all else. The Prophet (s) said to prioritize deen (religion). Additional factors: compatibility in values, education, and expectations; family background; physical attraction (looking at the potential spouse is recommended); financial stability of the husband; and mutual respect. Cultural compatibility helps but should not override religious suitability. Forced marriages are invalid.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['choosing','spouse','compatibility','deen']},
{id:'nk054',cat:'nikah',sub:'Temporary Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for temporary marriage (mutah)?',a:'Conditions include: (1) Specific time period must be stated, (2) Mahr must be specified, (3) The woman must not be currently married, (4) Iddah from previous marriage must be completed, (5) The formula must be recited, (6) The woman should be Muslim or from Ahlul Kitab, (7) Father\'s permission for a virgin is required (obligatory precaution). It is a legitimate institution in Shia fiqh with Quranic basis (4:24).',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['mutah','conditions','temporary','legitimate']},
{id:'nk055',cat:'nikah',sub:'Polygamy',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for polygamy?',a:'A man may marry up to four wives simultaneously with conditions: (1) Ability to treat all wives justly and equally — financially and in time spent, (2) Financial ability to maintain all households, (3) The Quran warns that if you cannot be just, then marry only one (4:3). Justice in emotional feelings is not required (as it is impossible) but justice in actions, time, and financial provision is obligatory.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['polygamy','justice','conditions','four']},
{id:'nk056',cat:'nikah',sub:'Interfaith',marja:'sistani',q:'Can a Muslim man marry a non-Muslim woman?',a:'A Muslim man may permanently marry a woman from Ahlul Kitab (Christian or Jewish) according to one scholarly opinion, though it is better avoided. Temporary marriage with Ahlul Kitab is more widely permitted. Marriage with polytheist or atheist women is not permitted. A Muslim woman cannot marry a non-Muslim man under any circumstances — this is a point of consensus.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['interfaith','ahlul kitab','christian','jewish']},
{id:'nk057',cat:'nikah',sub:'Wedding',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the recommended practices for Islamic weddings?',a:'Islam encourages celebrating marriage while maintaining modesty. Recommended: announcing the marriage publicly, hosting a walimah (feast), reciting the marriage sermon (khutbah), prayers for the couple, and gathering family and friends. The celebration should avoid: mixed-gender dancing, haram music, extravagance (israf), and alcohol. Simple but joyful celebrations are preferred over lavish events that burden families financially.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['wedding','walimah','celebration','modest']},
{id:'nk058',cat:'nikah',sub:'Domestic Issues',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Islam\'s position on domestic violence?',a:'Domestic violence is absolutely condemned in Islam. The Prophet (s) said the best among you are those who are best to their families. Physical abuse, emotional abuse, and verbal abuse all contradict Islamic teachings on marital kindness. A wife who faces abuse has the right to seek intervention from religious authorities, family mediation, legal protection, and divorce. Communities must take domestic violence seriously.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['violence','condemned','abuse','protection']},
{id:'nk059',cat:'nikah',sub:'Domestic Issues',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is Islam\'s position on domestic violence?',a:'Islam categorically prohibits all forms of domestic violence. The Quran instructs husbands to live with wives in kindness (4:19). The Prophet (s) never struck any of his wives and explicitly condemned those who did. Claiming religious justification for abuse is a gross distortion of Islam. Victims should seek help, and communities must provide support systems and hold abusers accountable.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['violence','prohibited','kindness','prophet']},
{id:'nk060',cat:'nikah',sub:'Engagement',marja:'makarem',q:'What is the ruling on engagement and pre-marriage interaction?',a:'Engagement (khitbah) is a promise of marriage, not a binding contract. During engagement, the couple are still non-mahram — full hijab and Islamic interaction rules apply. Brief, purposeful conversations to assess compatibility are permissible with proper boundaries. Physical contact and being alone together (khalwah) are not permitted. If either party breaks the engagement, gifts should be returned unless they were consumed.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['engagement','khitbah','interaction','boundaries']}
,
{id:'nk061',cat:'nikah',sub:'Consent',marja:'sistani',q:'Is the bride\'s consent required for marriage?',a:'The bride\'s consent is absolutely essential for a valid marriage — forced marriage is invalid in Islamic law. For a previously married woman (thayyib), her explicit verbal consent is required. For a virgin, her silence after being asked may be considered consent in some traditional interpretations, but explicit consent is strongly preferred. The father or paternal grandfather\'s permission for a virgin\'s first marriage is required by obligatory precaution in addition to her own consent.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['consent','required','bride','forced']},
{id:'nk062',cat:'nikah',sub:'Divorce Rights',marja:'makarem',q:'Can a woman include divorce conditions in the marriage contract?',a:'Yes, a woman can stipulate conditions in the marriage contract that give her the right to divorce under specific circumstances — such as if the husband takes a second wife, fails to provide maintenance, becomes abusive, or is absent for an extended period. These conditions are legally binding. This is an important tool for protecting women\'s rights within the Islamic framework and is strongly recommended.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['conditions','contract','divorce','protection']}
,
{id:'nk049',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the ruling on mahr (dowry) in marriage?',a:'Mahr is the wife\'s absolute right — it can be any amount or item of value agreed upon. It becomes her sole property upon marriage. The husband must pay it on demand (if prompt mahr) or at the agreed time. The marriage is valid even if mahr is not specified, but it must be determined. Excessive mahr that burdens the husband is discouraged. The mahr of Fatimah (a) was modest, setting an example.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['mahr','dowry','right','property']},
{id:'nk050',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mahr',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the ruling on mahr (dowry) in marriage?',a:'Mahr is obligatory and exclusively the wife\'s property. She can demand it at any time (if mahr al-muajjal). The husband cannot condition conjugal rights on mahr payment — these are separate obligations. Setting unreasonably high mahr creates social problems and is discouraged. The best mahr is one that is fair, manageable, and agreed upon happily by both sides.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['mahr','obligatory','fair','manageable']},
{id:'nk051',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the wife\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The wife\'s rights include: (1) Mahr payment, (2) Financial maintenance (nafaqah) — food, clothing, housing appropriate to her status, (3) Kind treatment and companionship, (4) Sexual fulfillment, (5) Not being harmed physically or emotionally, (6) Freedom to manage her own property and wealth, (7) Right to visit her family, (8) Right to education and personal development, (9) Right to work with mutual agreement, (10) Right to divorce through khul if the marriage fails.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['wife','rights','nafaqah','maintenance']},
{id:'nk052',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the wife\'s rights in marriage?',a:'Islam grants wives comprehensive rights: financial maintenance regardless of her own wealth, kind treatment (the Quran commands living with wives in kindness), mahr, inheritance, property rights, physical safety, and emotional respect. The husband cannot prevent her from fulfilling religious obligations. A wife who is mistreated has recourse to Islamic courts. The Prophet (s) said the best among you is the best to his wife.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['wife','rights','kindness','prophet']},
{id:'nk053',cat:'nikah',sub:'Rights',marja:'makarem',q:'What are the husband\'s rights in marriage?',a:'The husband\'s rights include: (1) Being the head of household in major decisions, (2) The wife should not leave the home without his consent for non-essential outings (this is debated in modern scholarship), (3) Intimate relations within reasonable bounds, (4) Respect and loyalty, (5) Maintaining household order. These rights are balanced by his extensive obligations: financial support, kindness, patience, and protecting the family\'s wellbeing.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['husband','rights','balanced','obligations']},
{id:'nk054',cat:'nikah',sub:'Conditions',marja:'sistani',q:'Can conditions be stipulated in the marriage contract?',a:'Yes, the wife can include conditions in the marriage contract. Common conditions: right to divorce, right to education, right to work, right to choose residence, right to travel, equal treatment with other wives (in polygamy), and delegation of divorce authority. These conditions are binding if agreed upon. A condition that contradicts the essence of marriage is void, but most practical conditions are valid.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['conditions','contract','stipulations','binding']},
{id:'nk055',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mixed Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'Can a Muslim man marry a non-Muslim woman?',a:'A Muslim man can permanently marry a Christian or Jewish woman (Ahl al-Kitab) according to many Shia scholars, though some consider it only permissible as temporary marriage. He cannot marry a polytheist or atheist. The children must be raised Muslim. A Muslim woman cannot marry a non-Muslim man under any circumstances — this is unanimous. In practice, interfaith marriages face significant challenges.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['interfaith','christian','jewish','prohibited']},
{id:'nk056',cat:'nikah',sub:'Mixed Marriage',marja:'khamenei',q:'Can a Muslim man marry a non-Muslim woman?',a:'Permanent marriage with Christian or Jewish women is permitted by some scholars but discouraged due to practical difficulties in maintaining Islamic household values. Temporary marriage with them is more widely accepted. A Muslim woman absolutely cannot marry a non-Muslim man. These rules protect the Islamic identity of the family and children. Conversion of the non-Muslim spouse is ideal but cannot be coerced.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['interfaith','marriage','conversion','identity']},
{id:'nk057',cat:'nikah',sub:'Polygamy',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for polygamy?',a:'A man may marry up to four wives simultaneously, but with strict conditions: (1) Ability to treat all wives equally in maintenance, time, and housing, (2) Financial capacity for multiple households, (3) The Quran itself warns \'if you fear you cannot be just, then marry only one\' (4:3). The first wife\'s consent, while not technically required, is strongly recommended. Injustice between wives is a grave sin.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['polygamy','conditions','equality','justice']},
{id:'nk058',cat:'nikah',sub:'Temporary Marriage',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the conditions for temporary marriage (mutah)?',a:'Conditions for mutah: (1) Specific time period must be stated, (2) Mahr must be specified, (3) The woman must not be currently married, (4) She must not be in iddah, (5) Both must consent freely, (6) The formula must be recited. A virgin requires her father\'s permission. The purpose of mutah in Shia jurisprudence is to provide a halal framework for relationships that cannot immediately be permanent.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['mutah','temporary','conditions','halal']},
{id:'nk059',cat:'nikah',sub:'Engagement',marja:'makarem',q:'What is permissible during the engagement period?',a:'Engagement is a promise of marriage, not marriage itself. The couple remain non-mahram until the marriage contract (aqd). During engagement: they should not be alone together, physical contact is not permissible, but seeing each other in family settings and communicating is fine. Some families perform the aqd early (with consummation delayed until the wedding), which resolves these restrictions.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['engagement','non-mahram','aqd','restrictions']},
{id:'nk060',cat:'nikah',sub:'Looking',marja:'sistani',q:'What is permissible to see of a potential spouse before marriage?',a:'Islam permits looking at a potential spouse before marriage to assess compatibility. The man may see: the woman\'s face, hands, and general appearance (with or without hijab according to some scholars, for this specific purpose). Some scholars extend this to hair and forearms. The looking should be purposeful (not prolonged or lustful) and the woman should also have the opportunity to see the man. This helps prevent regret after marriage.',source:'Istifta\'at',tags:['looking','spouse','permissible','compatibility']}
,
{id:'nk061',cat:'nikah',sub:'Spousal Relations',marja:'sistani',q:'What is Islam\'s view on intimacy between spouses?',a:'Marital intimacy is not only permissible but encouraged and rewarded in Islam. The Prophet (s) said intimacy with one\'s spouse is charity. Each spouse has the right to physical fulfillment. Guidelines: mutual pleasure and consideration, avoiding intercourse during menstruation, avoiding anal intercourse (haram on obligatory precaution), and maintaining privacy about intimate details. A healthy intimate relationship strengthens the marriage bond.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['intimacy','spouses','permissible','rewarded']},
{id:'nk062',cat:'nikah',sub:'Spousal Relations',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is Islam\'s view on intimacy between spouses?',a:'Islam has a positive view of marital intimacy — it is a natural need and a means of strengthening the marital bond. Both spouses have rights to fulfillment. The husband should not neglect his wife and vice versa. Restrictions are minimal: no intercourse during menstruation or post-natal bleeding, and avoidance of harmful practices. Communication between spouses about their needs is encouraged.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['intimacy','rights','communication','natural']},
{id:'nk063',cat:'nikah',sub:'Divorce Initiation',marja:'makarem',q:'Can a woman initiate divorce?',a:'While the standard divorce formula is pronounced by the husband, a woman has several pathways: (1) Khul — she offers compensation (usually returning mahr) for release, (2) Stipulated right — if the marriage contract includes a delegation of divorce authority to the wife, (3) Court dissolution — through a mujtahid for valid reasons (abuse, abandonment, non-support, harm). Islam does not trap women in harmful marriages.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['woman','divorce','khul','court']}
,
{id:'nk064',cat:'nikah',sub:'Wedding',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the recommended practices for Islamic weddings?',a:'Recommended practices: (1) Simplicity — the Prophet (s) encouraged modest weddings, (2) Walimah (wedding feast) — feeding guests is highly recommended, (3) Announcing the marriage publicly, (4) Reciting the marriage sermon (khutbah), (5) Expressing joy and celebration within Islamic limits, (6) Inviting family, friends, and neighbors. Extravagance, competition, unnecessary debt for weddings, and imitating non-Islamic wedding traditions should be avoided.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['wedding','simplicity','walimah','celebration']},
{id:'nk065',cat:'nikah',sub:'Compatibility',marja:'makarem',q:'What factors should be considered when choosing a spouse?',a:'The Prophet (s) said marry for religion above all. Key factors: (1) Religious commitment and practice, (2) Good character and ethics, (3) Compatibility in values and goals, (4) Family background, (5) Education and maturity, (6) Physical attraction (Islam permits seeing potential spouse), (7) Financial stability (for men as providers). Cultural compatibility, though not required, can ease married life. A pious spouse of modest means is better than a wealthy but irreligious one.',source:'Tawdih al-Masail',tags:['choosing','spouse','religion','compatibility']}
,
{id:'nk200',cat:'nikah',sub:'Maintenance',marja:'sistani',q:'What does the husband\'s maintenance obligation (nafaqah) include?',a:'Nafaqah includes: (1) Housing — appropriate to her social status, (2) Food — sufficient and of acceptable quality, (3) Clothing — suitable for the season and her standard, (4) Medical expenses, (5) Household furnishings, (6) Servant/help if she was accustomed to it before marriage. The standard is what is customary for a woman of her social level. If the husband fails to provide, she can take her case to an Islamic authority who can compel payment or grant divorce.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['nafaqah','maintenance','housing','obligation']},
{id:'nk201',cat:'nikah',sub:'Maintenance',marja:'khamenei',q:'What does the husband\'s maintenance obligation (nafaqah) include?',a:'The husband must provide: adequate housing, food, clothing, medical care, and other necessities appropriate to the wife\'s status. This is obligatory regardless of the wife\'s own wealth — even if she is wealthier than him. If the husband refuses to provide nafaqah, the wife can petition Islamic authorities. The obligation continues throughout the marriage and during iddah of revocable divorce. It ceases after irrevocable divorce except during pregnancy.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['nafaqah','obligatory','regardless','wealth']}
,
{id:'nk202',cat:'nikah',sub:'Problems',marja:'sistani',q:'What should couples do when facing marital difficulties?',a:'Islam provides a graduated approach: (1) Direct communication with kindness and patience, (2) Self-reflection — each person examining their own behavior, (3) Seeking help from trusted family members, (4) Consulting a knowledgeable scholar, (5) Professional counseling (permissible and encouraged), (6) Appointing mediators from each family (Quran 4:35), (7) Trial separation to gain perspective. Divorce is the final option only after all reconciliation efforts fail. Patience and compromise save most marriages.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['difficulties','counseling','mediators','patience']},
{id:'nk203',cat:'nikah',sub:'Problems',marja:'khamenei',q:'What should couples do when facing marital difficulties?',a:'Marital difficulties are normal — resolving them strengthens the bond. Steps: honest and respectful communication, avoiding blame and criticism, seeking family mediation, consulting scholars, and professional marriage counseling. Both spouses have rights and responsibilities — acknowledging shortcomings on both sides is essential. The Quran says to live together in kindness — if that\'s not possible, separate with dignity. But most problems are solvable with good faith effort.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['difficulties','communication','counseling','dignity']},
{id:'nk204',cat:'nikah',sub:'In-Laws',marja:'sistani',q:'What are the obligations regarding in-laws?',a:'Both spouses should maintain good relations with in-laws. The wife should respect the husband\'s parents and the husband should treat her family with kindness. However: the wife is not obligated to serve her in-laws (they are non-mahram), the husband cannot force his wife to live with his parents if she objects, and in-law disputes should be handled with wisdom. The husband must protect his wife from any mistreatment by his family.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['in-laws','obligations','respect','protection']},
{id:'nk205',cat:'nikah',sub:'In-Laws',marja:'khamenei',q:'What are the obligations regarding in-laws?',a:'Good relations with both families is important for marital harmony. Key points: the wife\'s primary home is with her husband (not his parents, unless she consents), she is not obligated to serve in-laws but kindness is rewarded, the husband should not allow his family to mistreat his wife, both families should facilitate the couple\'s independence. Cultural expectations about in-laws should not override the couple\'s Islamic rights.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['in-laws','independence','rights','cultural']}
,
{id:'nk300',cat:'nikah',sub:'Proposal',marja:'sistani',q:'What is the Islamic etiquette of proposing marriage?',a:'The man (or his family) approaches the woman\'s family with a formal proposal. Etiquette: investigate the person\'s religiousness and character beforehand, approach through proper channels (not secretly), the woman has full right to accept or refuse, no pressure should be applied, both parties should pray istikhara, and the woman\'s consent is absolutely essential — a marriage without the bride\'s genuine consent is invalid.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['proposal','consent','etiquette','istikhara']},
{id:'nk301',cat:'nikah',sub:'Proposal',marja:'khamenei',q:'What is the Islamic etiquette of proposing marriage?',a:'Marriage begins with a dignified proposal process. Both families should investigate compatibility — religion, character, family background. The woman\'s consent is non-negotiable — no father, brother, or guardian can force a marriage without her genuine agreement. Islam permits the couple to see each other and have supervised conversations before deciding. Istikhara is recommended but should not override clear compatibility or incompatibility.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['proposal','consent','investigation','compatible']},
{id:'nk302',cat:'nikah',sub:'Duty',marja:'sistani',q:'Is marriage obligatory in Islam?',a:'Marriage is mustahab muakkad (strongly recommended) and becomes obligatory when one fears falling into sin without it. The Prophet (s) said: marriage is my Sunnah, whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not from me. Remaining unmarried by choice when one has the means and ability contradicts Islamic guidance. However, marriage is not obligatory for those who cannot afford it — they should maintain chastity through fasting and patience until able.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['marriage','recommended','obligatory','sunnah']},
{id:'nk303',cat:'nikah',sub:'Duty',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is marriage obligatory in Islam?',a:'Marriage is one of the most emphasized recommendations in Islam, becoming obligatory when remaining single risks sinful behavior. The Prophet (s) said when a person marries, they complete half their religion. Young people should be facilitated to marry — communities should reduce financial barriers (excessive mahr, extravagant weddings). Delaying marriage without reason while having the means contradicts the Prophet\'s guidance.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['marriage','half religion','barriers','youth']},
{id:'nk304',cat:'nikah',sub:'Age',marja:'sistani',q:'Is there a minimum age for marriage in Islam?',a:'Islamic law permits marriage after puberty (bulugh), which is 9 lunar years for girls and 15 for boys. However, consummation requires physical and mental maturity — not merely reaching the minimum age of puberty. Many scholars emphasize that marriage should occur when both parties are genuinely ready — physically, emotionally, and financially. A guardian\'s permission is required for a virgin girl\'s marriage regardless of age.',source:'Minhaj al-Salihin',tags:['age','puberty','maturity','guardian']},
{id:'nk305',cat:'nikah',sub:'Age',marja:'khamenei',q:'Is there a minimum age for marriage in Islam?',a:'While Islamic law sets puberty as the minimum, modern circumstances require considering readiness comprehensively. Physical maturity alone is insufficient — emotional, psychological, and financial readiness matter. Very early marriages can harm both parties if they are not truly prepared. The marja\'s permission and family\'s wise judgment should guide the timing. The trend toward later marriage in many societies reflects practical realities that Islam accommodates.',source:'Ajwibat al-Istifta\'at',tags:['age','readiness','comprehensive','wisdom']}
];